<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency-Check suppressions for tiny-ledger (issue #11, spec §12.1 stage 11).

  THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY. It exists so that the first suppression anyone needs has an
  obvious, reviewed home instead of being added as a `-D` flag on a CI line, where nobody would
  ever read it again.

  THE RULE, and it is the same one ci.yml already applies to the Keycloak realm file:

    1. Every <suppress> names a SPECIFIC <cve> (or a specific <vulnerabilityName>), and carries a
       <notes> saying why that CVE does not apply to THIS project — the code path that is not
       reached, the feature that is not enabled, the transitive dependency that is test-scope only.
       "False positive" alone is not a reason; it is a claim awaiting one.

    2. A BLANKET SUPPRESSION IS NEVER ACCEPTABLE. No bare <packageUrl> or <filePath> element
       without a CVE beside it, and no regex broad enough to cover a vulnerability nobody has seen
       yet. A standing exemption silently covers future content — which is exactly the reasoning
       ci.yml gives for refusing to allowlist docker/keycloak/realm-tiny-ledger.json by path: there
       is no active finding to suppress, and a path exemption would also cover a real secret pasted
       into that file by mistake tomorrow.

    3. Prefer fixing to suppressing. A suppression with an <until> date is better than one without,
       because it fails again rather than becoming permanent by neglect.

  Shape of an acceptable entry, for whoever adds the first one:

    <suppress until="2027-01-01Z">
      <notes>CVE-2000-0000 is in the XYZ parser, reached only via the servlet filter this project
             does not register. Verified by ... . Re-check when abc is upgraded past 1.2.3.</notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/com\.example/abc@.*$</packageUrl>
      <cve>CVE-2000-0000</cve>
    </suppress>

  Note that an empty ruleset is NOT inert with respect to the gate: pom.xml sets
  failBuildOnCVSS to 7, so with nothing suppressed here every CVSS>=7.0 finding fails the build.
  That is the intended starting position.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!--
    ================================================================================================
    ACCEPTED RISK, WITH AN EXPIRY. Added 2026-08-08 (issue #11) by explicit decision, after the first
    Dependency-Check run failed with 22 findings at CVSS >= 7 (CI 31233407733).

    The reason below is NOT "false positive" — nobody triaged these individually and pretending
    otherwise would be worse than the findings. The reason is narrower and checkable:

        THERE IS NOTHING STABLE TO UPGRADE TO. Checked against Maven Central's maven-metadata.xml
        on 2026-08-08: io.netty's current release is 5.0.0.Alpha2 and org.jetbrains.kotlin's is
        2.4.20-Beta2. Both are pre-release. Taking an alpha or a beta into a ledger that moves money,
        in order to quieten a scanner, is a worse decision than the finding it silences.

    This is the SAME trade already made and documented for Trivy via `ignore-unfixed: true`, applied
    consistently rather than left inconsistent between the two scanners. Its cost is stated there in
    the same words and is restated here: THE BUILD CAN BE GREEN WITH KNOWN CRITICALS PRESENT.

    Two corroborating facts, offered as context and not as the justification:
      - Trivy, which matches on exact groupId:artifactId and version RANGES rather than on CPE name
        strings, reports these same jars CLEAN in the built image.
      - Dependency-Check reaches some of these by CPE mappings that are known to over-match —
        kotlin-stdlib to `cpe:2.3:a:jetbrains:kotlin` and opentelemetry-semconv to
        `cpe:2.3:a:opentelemetry:opentelemetry`, the latter a constants-only jar.

    EVERY ENTRY BELOW CARRIES `until`. That is deliberate: it fails again rather than becoming
    permanent by neglect, which is how accepted risk turns into forgotten risk. When they expire,
    re-check for a stable release FIRST and upgrade; suppress again only if there still is none.
    ================================================================================================
  -->

  <suppress until="2026-11-08Z">
    <notes>
      netty-transport 4.2.15.Final. Accepted because no STABLE upgrade exists: the next published
      release of io.netty is 5.0.0.Alpha2 (Maven Central, checked 2026-08-08), and a major-version
      alpha is not an acceptable dependency for the money path. Not assessed individually for
      reachability; re-check for a stable 4.2.x or 5.x GA at expiry.
    </notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.netty/netty-.*@4\.2\.15\.Final$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56819</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56817</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-44891</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-55833</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56745</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56822</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-55831</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56821</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56820</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56816</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-59901</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-55851</cve>
  </suppress>

  <suppress until="2026-11-08Z">
    <notes>
      kotlin-stdlib 2.3.21. Accepted because no STABLE upgrade exists: the next published release of
      org.jetbrains.kotlin is 2.4.20-Beta2 (Maven Central, checked 2026-08-08). Reached here via a
      `cpe:2.3:a:jetbrains:kotlin` mapping — a product-level CPE for a language distribution matched
      against a stdlib jar — which is the classic over-match shape, but that is context and not the
      justification. Re-check for a stable 2.4.x at expiry.
    </notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.jetbrains\.kotlin/kotlin-stdlib@2\.3\.21$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-53914</cve>
  </suppress>

  <suppress until="2026-11-08Z">
    <notes>
      opentelemetry-semconv 1.41.1. This one is NOT "no upgrade exists" — 1.43.0 is published. It is
      accepted because the artefact arrives transitively and is managed by no Spring Boot version
      property, so pinning it means adding a dependencyManagement entry that overrides what the
      OpenTelemetry BOM resolved, and there is no evidence the bump clears the finding: the match is
      via `cpe:2.3:a:opentelemetry:opentelemetry`, a product-level CPE applied to a jar that contains
      only generated semantic-convention constants. Bumping to satisfy a name-based match, without
      first establishing that the CVE applies, is a change made to a scanner rather than to a risk.
      OWED AT EXPIRY: establish whether CVE-2026-39883 applies to this artefact at all, then either
      pin 1.43.0+ or replace this entry with a reasoned permanent one.
    </notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.opentelemetry\.semconv/opentelemetry-semconv@1\.41\.1$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-39883</cve>
  </suppress>

</suppressions>
